{"kind":"report","text":"Completed the read-only defensive release-readiness audit at immutable head aff0fa37c970086b6bf985541969c1ff63d83f51. The replacement record inventories every production package and external boundary, consolidates 14 directly traced findings with impact/effort/risk/confidence, records verified false positives and trust assumptions, and reports successful proportional gates, govulncheck, npm audit, and Gitleaks with precise limitations. No source, index, branch, setting, remote, or deployment state changed.","body":{"false_positives":"Intent parsing is bounded and canonical: internal/intent/intent.go:16-41,:69-120,:136-151 plus focused aggregate/string/causal/signature tests.\nPayload tree shape/size is checked at internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:419-458; checkpoint reads are capped at 256 MiB and covered by malformed/bound tests.\nHTTP mutation input is capped at 2 MiB with unknown fields rejected at internal/service/server.go:298-304; mutation guards block normal cross-origin/safelisted requests.\nThe CLI resident path already has loopback, redirect, timeout, response-size, trailing-JSON, and frontier validation at cmd/gs/main.go:658-760; the weakness is the separate MCP path.\nThe listener is loopback-only (cmd/gs/main.go:832-869), and docs/reference.md:369-407 explicitly documents trusted-local/no-HTTP-auth behavior.\nNexus session IDs are credentials, but only unrelated random handles are published (internal/nexus/nexus.go:28-50 and focused nondisclosure/nonderivability tests).\nActor/config key material is written mode 0600 (internal/app/app.go:350-393); temporary allowed-signers files are mode 0600 (internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:476-490).\nThe workroom fold discards payload and attachments after decode (internal/workroom/fold.go:229-234 and focused test). React rendering and legacy HTML escaping revealed no direct XSS path.\nREADME.md:6-9 already labels this a technical preview rather than making a blanket production-security claim. No dependency scanner or secret scanner finding was observed.\nCommit attribution “Hugh Pyle \u003chughpyle@gmail.com\u003e” is explicitly accepted by the user; it is not a secret/attribution incident and no history rewrite is needed.","findings_critical_high":"CRITICAL — Git replacement objects are not disabled. Evidence: internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:57-66 builds every Git subprocess from the inherited environment without GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS; trust/object reads at :275-296 and :363-489 use that runner (direct execs :372-374 and :422-424). Impact: a local replacement ref can substitute an object at the immutable sequence verification/signature boundary. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.\nHIGH — executable Git configuration is not quarantined. Evidence: internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:57-66 inherits os.Environ; signing :258-266 and verification :486-490 set format/key/allowedSigners but do not neutralize gpg.ssh.program or system/global/repository configuration. Impact: a security-sensitive sign/verify can invoke a configured program with process privileges. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: high because portable signing behavior changes. Confidence: high.\nHIGH — loopback custody is exposed to Host/DNS-rebinding confusion. Evidence: internal/service/server.go:64-80 installs no Host allowlist; :283-295 compares Origin host with the request's attacker-influenced Host; cmd/gs/main.go:832-869 enforces only a loopback listen address; internal/service/ui.go:97-125 lets /v0/act use session custody. Impact: a malicious same-origin hostname rebound to loopback can cross the custody boundary and sign as a locally held actor. Estimated fix: small. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.\nHIGH — revoked/non-live actors can author new effective statements. Evidence: internal/workroom/fold.go:305-344 has no record-author membership/liveness check (only request addressee validation at :313-315); kernel admission is the static local allowlist in internal/app/app.go:734-741. Impact: a revoked key can still create effective assertions, artifacts, promises, or reports when the statement-specific shape rules pass. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: high because fold compatibility/profile behavior changes. Confidence: high.\nHIGH — resident dedup/checkpoint state retains payloads and can retry failed oversized checkpoints on every append. Evidence: internal/kernel/kernel.go:547-568 stores full Events in dedup/checkpointEvents after local append, while the full scan strips payload at :833-840; checkpointEvents is cloned/appended at :493-503 and :557-568; a failed due write does not advance the attempt. internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:281-301 serializes every payload/attachment and :17-23 caps the blob at 256 MiB. Impact: memory amplification plus repeated O(history) serialization after an oversize/write failure. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: high because restart/cache correctness is involved. Confidence: high.\nHIGH — live/service resources are insufficiently bounded. Evidence: internal/nexus/nexus.go:117-163 grows presence/conversations/about/participants while only history is capped; sessions :191-221 and conversations/frames :302-316,:384-450 grow while sessions live. cmd/gs/main.go:853 sets only ReadHeaderTimeout; internal/service/server.go:146-169 holds one polling wait loop per request. Impact: a reachable local client can consume memory, goroutines, and connections. Estimated fix: medium-to-large. Regression risk: medium-to-high. Confidence: high. Scope correction: HTTP mutation bodies are already bounded to 2 MiB and strictly decoded at internal/service/server.go:298-304.","findings_medium":"MEDIUM — the MCP resident client is weaker than the CLI resident client. Evidence: cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:118 uses \u0026http.Client{}; :506-523 performs an unbounded JSON decode with default timeout/redirect behavior; :491-503 concatenates the URL. The CLI validates loopback at cmd/gs/main.go:658-672 and refuses redirects, applies time/response caps, rejects trailing JSON, and checks the frontier at :675-760. Impact: MCP can hang, over-allocate, follow redirects, or accept fabricated resident data; fallback occurs only on transport errors. Estimated fix: small-to-medium. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — adversarial projection/status work has quadratic or whole-history costs. Evidence: internal/workroom/fold.go:655-671 scans all predecessor artifacts for every same-path artifact; internal/app/app.go:800-813 reprojects complete state; internal/statusview/view.go:109-179 builds full lists before presentation caps. Impact: signed high-cardinality history can drive latency and allocation. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — malformed local checkpoint depth can overflow/panic before authentication. Evidence: internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:131-155 computes stored.Depth+1 and indexes sequence inside that loop; load validates structure before verifying the checkpoint signature at :102-111. Impact: an extreme local checkpoint can panic a resident/auditor instead of producing bounded fallback. Estimated fix: small. Regression risk: low. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — terminal controls and Git graph framing are collision-prone. Evidence: internal/statusview/view.go:84-95 normalizes whitespace but leaves ESC/bidi/C0 controls and :224-241 renders request text; internal/workroom/render.go:123-125 escapes only '|'. internal/gitstore/graph.go:30-49 frames Git output with US/RS bytes that commit text may contain, then skips malformed rows. Impact: terminal/status spoofing and graph row corruption or omission. Estimated fix: small-to-medium. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — custody dispute ordering counts non-owners. Evidence: internal/custody/custody.go:109-116 marks any more-than-one completed saga disputed before owner validity is checked at :118-128. Impact: a non-owner completed saga can dispute a legitimate transfer. Estimated fix: small-to-medium. Regression risk: high because custody semantics change. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — browser security headers are absent. Evidence: internal/service/ui.go:20-35 supplies Cache-Control only; internal/service/server.go:307-315 supplies Content-Type/Cache-Control only; no CSP frame-ancestors, X-Frame-Options, nosniff, or referrer policy is installed. Impact: the resident UI can be framed/clickjacked, compounding trusted-loopback custody. Estimated fix: small. Regression risk: low-to-medium. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — optional Gitea development compose binds first-run service to all interfaces. Evidence: spike/compose.yaml:9-10 maps \"3300:3000\" without a loopback host; spike/FORGE.md:12-16 requires manual UI/API setup after start. Impact: a LAN peer can reach or race a first-run development forge. Estimated fix: small. Regression risk: low. Confidence: high.\nMEDIUM — release policy/CI/build provenance is incomplete. Evidence: aff0fa37 has no .github/ or SECURITY.md; internal/service/ui.go:14-18 embeds committed uidist. A locked clean npm build passed, but index.html asset names changed; committed JS index-DNvpBaaB.js and rebuilt index-DGcBVa3B.js were byte-identical (SHA-256 14760f9d…, 308200 bytes), while committed CSS index-3LzhWxW5.css (SHA-256 177154d44785…, 37917 bytes) differed from rebuilt index-BrtnaYK-.css (SHA-256 c05067ea1843…, 72024 bytes). Impact: no continuous release/security gate or reporting channel, and reviewed UI source does not reproduce the served CSS/embed. Estimated fix: medium. Regression risk: medium. Confidence: high.","head":"Reviewed immutable source head aff0fa37c970086b6bf985541969c1ff63d83f51 only. No source, branch, index, setting, remote, or deployment state was changed.","inventory":"Production Go packages and boundaries: cmd/gs (CLI; filesystem/repository; Git subprocess; loopback resident; remote fetch/merge); cmd/gitseq-mcp (stdio JSON-RPC/MCP; loopback HTTP; local fallback; actor custody); internal/app (workspace/config/key custody; worktree inspection; kernel/fold join); internal/gitstore (Git and ssh-keygen subprocesses; object/ref/sign/verify trust boundary); internal/intent (bounded canonical CBOR, Ed25519, envelope); internal/kernel (immutable sequence verification, CAS append, checkpoint/restart); internal/workroom (schema/fold/authority/status/provenance); internal/nexus (leased sessions and signed transient frames); internal/service (loopback HTTP/API and embedded UI); internal/statusview (bounded CLI/MCP presentation); internal/custody (experimental cross-log custody fold). internal/domain has test fixtures but no production Go files.\nBrowser and operational surface: ui/src is a same-origin React client using HTTP and personal localStorage; spike/cmd/* and spike/compose.yaml are adversarial/optional Docker-Gitea evidence; go.mod/go.sum, ui/package-lock.json, Makefile, committed uidist, README/docs/SKILL/AGENTS are release/build/documentation surfaces. aff0fa37 contains neither .github/ nor SECURITY.md.","limitations":"Defensive read-only audit only: no exploit development, browser automation or real DNS-rebinding exercise, live multi-user load attack, remote push/settings mutation, OS/container/Gitea deployment inspection, or exhaustive cryptographic/side-channel review.\nScope is immutable aff0fa37 only; later repair/public branches were not assessed. Source tracing and passing tests are not formal proof.\ngovulncheck covers reachable Go call paths against a database last updated 2026-07-27; it does not cover npm packages, runtime/configuration flaws, or future advisories. npm audit covers lockfile dependency advisories, not application logic/runtime exposure.\nGitleaks v8.30.1 used default rules and --log-opts=aff0fa37...; it reported 109 commits/6.58 MB while Git counted 151 reachable commits. Default Git scanning does not imply archive traversal, exhaustive arbitrary-secret discovery, future-rule coverage, or attribution validation.\nThe clean UI build completed but did not reproduce committed asset names/CSS bytes, so the earlier clean-rebuild claim is expressly withdrawn and replaced by the measured result above.\nThe root worktree was in another actor's conflicted merge. All reads/tests used immutable objects and the existing clean release-readiness worktree/archive; the root index was not touched.","trust_assumptions":"The OS account, common Git directory, and .git/gitseq actor/sequencer key custody are trusted; a key custodian can sign.\nInstalled git/ssh-keygen, Go/Node/npm toolchains, and vulnerability/secret databases and rules are trusted. Repository/system Git configuration is not accepted as executable signing authority; that gap is finding 2.\nLoopback clients/local processes are trusted by current design and HTTP has no authentication; docs/reference.md:384-407 says any loopback-reachable client can select a held actor and sign.\nForge repository ACL is the deployment boundary; there is no per-ref confidentiality (spike/FORGE.md:19-22). Signatures prove key possession, not human intention; the fold supplies semantic authority.\nBrowser localStorage is personal/best-effort state, not durable authority.","verification":"At aff0fa37: go vet ./... passed; go test ./... passed; go test -race ./... passed; go build ./cmd/gs ./cmd/gitseq-mcp ./spike/cmd/gitseq-report ./spike/cmd/gitseq-spike passed.\nAt aff0fa37/ui: npm ci passed (87 packages, 88 audited); npm test passed 34/34; npm audit --audit-level=low reported 0 vulnerabilities; npm run build passed, with the non-reproducible uidist result detailed above.\ngovulncheck succeeded: go run golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@latest ./... reported “No vulnerabilities found.” Tool/version context: Go go1.26.5, govulncheck v1.6.0, database https://vuln.go.dev updated 2026-07-27 20:14:16 UTC.\nGitleaks succeeded: go run github.com/zricethezav/gitleaks/v8@v8.30.1 git --no-banner --redact --log-opts=aff0fa37... /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/release-readiness exited 0 and reported no leaks, 109 commits scanned, about 6.58 MB. git independently reports 151 reachable commits and 1499 reachable objects at aff0fa37; the scanner's smaller commit metric is disclosed rather than treated as one separately scanned record per reachable commit.\nFocused guard evidence checked includes TestIntentBoundsEveryStringAndCausalCount, TestEnvelopeParserUsesExplicitAggregateBound, signature/binding tests, checkpoint bound/corruption tests, TestSessionIdentifiersAreNeverPublished, TestHandleIsNotDerivable..., and the workroom test that discards decoded payload/attachments."}}